type: software
title: "Which dice colourings make the number of colours seen most uncertain? Fair coins, hub designs and a local limit theorem for doubly stochastic occupancy"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. Colour the faces of m fair dice with n colours, never repeating a colour on one die, roll them all and count the distinct colours that appear. For fair coins, every sufficiently large palette (an ineffective threshold, at least ten) and every m >= n, the unique entropy-maximising design is a star: one colour on every coin, paired once with each other colour; up to three extra coins duplicate distinct pairs and four or more duplicate one pair. Three colours are solved for every number of coins. For every number of faces r >= 2 and m = n, explicit hub designs beat every evenly spread design by at least (1/2) log 1.5 nats once n is large, so Pelekis's even-spread conjecture and circular maximality fail for every r; for 2- to 8-faced dice this is certified exactly up to 120 colours. Pelekis's Conjecture 4.1 is proved with an absolute constant. The tool is a local limit theorem, with Edgeworth expansions and the squared-skewness entropy correction, for the number of distinct values when each row of a doubly stochastic matrix is sampled once."
